#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167642} :activity/id #uuid "7042cf87-d987-5e5d-80bf-d9af04e62830" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049358 :topic/id #uuid "eea04b94-4b8a-5afe-a7ad-7279c5cf1588" :topic/math-academy-id 3282} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167642 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168071 :tutorial/content "A discrete random variable $X$ follows a **Poisson distribution** (pronounced \"pwaas-on\") if it has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{\\lambda^x e^{-\\lambda}}{x!}, \\qquad x=0,1,2,\\ldots\n$$\n\nThe **rate parameter** $\\lambda$ is a positive real number, and\n\n$$\ne\\approx 2.71828\n$$\n\nis Euler's number.\n\nIf a random variable $X$ follows a Poisson distribution, we write\n\n$$\nX \\sim \\textrm{Po}(\\lambda)\n$$\n\nThe Poisson distribution models the number of independent events $X$ that occur within a fixed interval of space or time. The parameter $\\lambda$ represents the mean number of events that occur in an interval (we'll justify this in a future lesson).\n\nFor example, suppose that an average of\n\n$$\n\\lambda={\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nbuses stop at a particular bus shelter every $10$ minutes. The number of buses that stop at the shelter in a randomly selected $10$-minute period can be modeled by a Poisson random variable\n\n$$\nX \\sim \\textrm{Po}({\\color{blue}2})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that\n\n$$\nX={\\color{red}3}\n$$\n\nbuses will stop at the shelter in the next $10$ minutes is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = 3) &= f(3) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2^{3}e^{-2}}{3!} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.1804\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nWe will learn more about modeling situations using Poisson random variables shortly. But for now, let's concentrate on computing probabilities for Poisson random variables." :tutorial/id #uuid "6fd6a3d4-a095-5f17-8527-5e6b17f4d813" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5030 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6e446eaa-af47-5f7a-a199-28e77e17fe31" :step/math-academy-id 31456 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167653}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167653 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166816 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166654 :question/id #uuid "fba5b411-86fe-5d2f-85ee-87cfa1e96d83" :question/math-academy-id "e-8465"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74510348-04f1-570b-b3f7-5fcb1ed1beef" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049619}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670455} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670576}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability at a Point"} :step/id #uuid "036aa9a7-bb92-5d00-ba65-ec3f305143b1" :step/math-academy-id 31518 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167654}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167654 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166817 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166655 :question/id #uuid "62d1e798-9a57-5657-9a6d-8fb70bff0ccd" :question/math-academy-id "e-8466"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "540361c1-5d4e-5b88-a295-f606f5518f38" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167486}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over a Bounded Interval: Lower Bound is Zero"} :step/id #uuid "066e3107-bb37-5eb1-95af-02181840af06" :step/math-academy-id 31519 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167655}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166818 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166656 :question/id #uuid "c89b3081-38d7-5430-b912-82f84ef532ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-8467"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21835800-1a05-56c1-a5ee-b8c2e1df2273" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167487}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over a Bounded Interval: Lower Bound is Not Zero"} :step/id #uuid "0173dbc0-d2b4-5629-a48d-de97e9d25b57" :step/math-academy-id 31520 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167656}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166819 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166657 :question/id #uuid "d1e693a2-073f-5f8c-98fd-3b89496521d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-8468"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6219a2a-8c28-57c1-a4ad-0ede62529376" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049642}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167488}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Unbounded Interval Using the Complement"} :step/id #uuid "1e8e01c3-7ec6-5992-b850-be3724d44357" :step/math-academy-id 31521 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167720}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167720 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168107 :tutorial/content "Recall that for a function $f(x)$ with support $S$ to be a valid probability mass function for a discrete random variable $X$, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $0 \\leq f(x) \\leq 1$ for all $x$ in $S$\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x) = 1$\n\nLet's check these two conditions for the Poisson distribution. First, let's write down the pmf of a Poisson random variable:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{\\lambda^x e^{-\\lambda}}{x!}, \\qquad x=0,1,2,3, \\ldots\n$$\n\nWe'll also make use of the following result:\n\n$$\ne^{\\lambda} = \\sum\\limits_{x = 0}^\\infty \\dfrac{\\lambda^x}{x!}\n$$\n\nYou'll learn where this result comes from when you study calculus. But for now, let's assume it's true.\n\nUsing this result, we can show that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x) = 1\n$$\n\nis true as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(x ∈ S)f(x) &= ∑_(x = 0)^(∞)f(x) \\\\\n&= ∑_(x = 0)^(∞)\\frac{λ^{x}e^{- λ}}{x!} \\\\\n&= e^{- λ}∑_(x = 0)^(∞)\\frac{λ^{x}}{x!} \\\\\n&= e^{- λ} \\cdot e^{λ} \\\\\n&= e^{0} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we note that\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{\\lambda^x e^{-\\lambda}}{x!} \\geq 0\n$$\n\nfor all $x$ because $\\lambda^x$, $e^{-\\lambda}$, and $x$! are all positive. Furthermore, because $f(x)$ is non-negative and the sum of all values is $1$, we must have\n\n$$\nf(x) \\leq 1\n$$\n\nfor all $x$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n0 \\leq f(x) \\leq 1\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $f(x)$ is a valid probability mass function." :tutorial/id #uuid "32806a77-5615-54c5-adb0-f441385189a2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5357 :tutorial/title "Justification That the Poisson Distribution Forms a Probability Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "78939e0d-595e-5a87-8656-d3e42cf8f110" :step/math-academy-id 33487}] :activity/title "The Poisson Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167044}]}
